;; FH-ROOT-002 — a compatible hot reload keeps the mounted OCCURRENCE.
;;
;; A declared view is a descriptor VALUE, so a hot reload mints a NEW
;; descriptor object for the redefined view. But the value it carries holds
;; the same qualified `:view-id`, and the root's identity — its `:root-id` —
;; is derived from that id, so the reload's root-id is unchanged. The
;; body/generation churn is an internal fact of the descriptor object,
;; never part of the identity.
;;
;; Two things follow, and BOTH are the law:
;;
;;   1. re-mounting the same root into the same container is idempotent — it
;;      RE-RENDERS the existing host root instead of allocating a second one.
;;      A second host root on one container is a React error and would tear
;;      the whole tree down and reseed it.
;;   2. the boundary UNDER that root is the one React already mounted. The
;;      interpreted emitter caches its React component under the qualified
;;      view id, so a compatible redefinition hands React the type it is
;;      already reconciling and the mounted occurrence survives: the same
;;      Fiber, the same ViewCell, the same DOM nodes, and the browser state
;;      those nodes hold.
;;
;; Claim 1 alone is not the law and must not be mistaken for it. An identical
;; host root object says nothing about what hangs beneath it — a fresh
;; component TYPE below a reused root unmounts and remounts the whole
;; boundary, and the new text still appears. So the occurrence is proven with
;; state React preserves ONLY across a stable component type: an UNCONTROLLED
;; input's value and its focus. A remount answers a different DOM node,
;; reseeded from the new body and unfocused.
;;
;; The reload is SIMULATED by two descriptors that share one view-id and
;; differ only in their body — exactly how a redefinition presents (the
;; same qualified identity, a fresh body generation), and how the donor's
;; `register-view-generation!` models a reload without redefining a var.
{:fh/id "FH-ROOT-002"
 :fh/law
 "A root's occurrence identity is its derived root-id — the mounted view's qualified id, which a redefinition does not move — so a compatible redefinition re-renders the existing host root through the boundary React already mounted: the reloaded body renders and the mounted occurrence below it, down to the browser state its DOM nodes hold, is not reseeded. A redefinition whose hook skeleton moved is not served that boundary."

 :root-id :re-frame.freehand.root-hmr/app

 ;; the two body generations of the reloaded view — same view-id, a
 ;; body-only change
 :before "before"
 :after  "after"

 ;; the uncontrolled input's SEED in each generation. The seed moves WITH
 ;; the body, so a remount is not a silent no-op: it puts `seed-after` on
 ;; the screen in place of whatever was typed.
 :seed-before "seed-before"
 :seed-after  "seed-after"

 ;; what is typed into the LIVE occurrence before the reload — DOM state
 ;; React preserves only while the component type is stable
 :typed "typed"

 ;; the reload re-renders the EXISTING host root (identity preserved, no reseed)
 :same-host-root true

 ;; ... and keeps the mounted OCCURRENCE beneath it. Each claim is read back
 ;; off the live document, not off an element tree.
 :occurrence-preserved {:same-input-node true   ; the same DOM node, not an equal one
                        :typed-value     true   ; still carrying what was typed
                        :focus           true}  ; and still the document's active element

 ;; The emitter's stable boundary is what makes that possible: ONE cache
 ;; entry per qualified view id however many generations a session reloads,
 ;; and a body revision that advances at the RELOAD seam — once per new
 ;; body, never per walk — so an in-flight render of the old body is stale
 ;; at commit and publishes nothing.
 :boundary {:entries 1 :revision-after-reload 1 :revision-after-rewalk 1}

 ;; The other arm. A redefinition whose SHELL SIGNATURE moved — the hook
 ;; skeleton the emitter would wrap it in — is NOT served the cached
 ;; boundary. Promotion between modes is the reachable case: the same
 ;; qualified id redeclared `{:compiled true}`. The interpreted boundary
 ;; must not render it, and today the compiled React lowering answers that
 ;; with its own loud refusal rather than a remount.
 :incompatible {:error :rf.error/view-lowering-unavailable}

 ;; non-vacuity control: a SECOND root, in a different container, is a
 ;; genuinely new root — its host root is NOT the reloaded one, so the reuse
 ;; above is a real decision rather than an always-true tautology.
 ;;
 ;; It carries a `:disambiguator`, and it has to. Root-ids are page-unique
 ;; identity, so the same derived id in a second container is the
 ;; duplicate-root-id refusal, not a second root — which is exactly why the
 ;; control has to say what distinguishes the two sites.
 :fresh-container-host-root :distinct
 :fresh-container-opts {:disambiguator :control}}
